<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  14.1 Functions of Several Variables
       (including the level-surfaces material)
-->
<section xml:id="chapter-functions-several-variables" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Functions of Several Variables</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Evaluate a function <m>z = f(x,y)</m> of two variables, describe its
domain as a region of the plane, and recognize its graph as a surface in
space.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find and sketch the level curves <m>f(x,y) = c</m> of a function of two
variables, and read a contour map.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Explain the relationship between the level curves drawn on the surface
and the contour map drawn in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Describe the level surfaces <m>f(x,y,z) = c</m> of a function of three
variables.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
So far we have studied functions that take one real number as input and
return one real number as output. Many quantities, however, depend on
more than one input: the temperature at a point of a metal plate depends
on two coordinates, and the temperature in a room depends on three. In
this section we study functions of two variables, whose graphs are
surfaces in space, and we develop two ways of visualizing them, namely
level curves and contour maps. At the end we look at functions of three
variables, which we visualize by their level surfaces.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-fsv-functions-two-variables">
<title>Functions of Two Variables</title>

<p>
As an example consider <m>z = f(x,y) = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m>. This function
takes a pair of real numbers <m>x, y \in \R</m> as its input and as an
output gives a real number <m>z \in \R</m>. For instance, consider the
point <m>P_0(0.5, 0.5)</m>, which is inside the disk
<m>x^2 + y^2 \le 1</m>. For <m>P_0(0.5,0.5)</m> as an input, the output
is
<md>
z = f(0.5, 0.5) = 1 - (0.5)^2 - (0.5)^2 = 0.5.
</md>
<xref ref="fig-fsv-mapping"/> shows this as a mapping: each point of the
disk is sent to a number on the <m>z</m>-axis.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-fsv-mapping">
<caption>
The function <m>f</m> takes a point <m>(x,y)</m> of the disk
<m>x^2+y^2 \le 1</m> and returns the single number <m>z = f(x,y)</m>. The
point <m>P_0(0.5,0.5)</m> is sent to <m>f(0.5,0.5) = 0.5</m>.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-fsv-mapping">
<diagram dimensions="(600,270)" margins="12">
<definition>bt(t) = ((1-t)^2*1.05 + 2*t*(1-t)*4.0 + t^2*7.0, (1-t)^2*1.25 + 2*t*(1-t)*3.15 + t^2*0.42)</definition>
<definition>bb(t) = ((1-t)^2*0.28 + 2*t*(1-t)*3.3 + t^2*6.55, (1-t)^2*(-0.62) + 2*t*(1-t)*(-2.05) + t^2*0.05)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.6,-2.6,9.4,3.5)">
<circle at="fm-disk" center="(0,0)" radius="1.9"
        fill="#7F7F7F" fill-opacity="0.28" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2.5"/>
<vector at="fm-xaxis" v="(4.15,0)" tail="(-2.1,0)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="fm-yaxis" v="(0,5.0)" tail="(0,-2.2)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="fm-zaxis" v="(4.0,0)" tail="(5.0,0.25)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="fm-top" function="bt" domain="(0,0.95)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="fm-top-arrow" v="(0.300,-0.261)" tail="(6.700,0.681)"
        stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="fm-bot" function="bb" domain="(0,0.95)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="fm-bot-arrow" v="(0.324,0.201)" tail="(6.226,-0.151)"
        stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="2"/>
<point at="fm-P0" p="(0.956,0.956)" fill="#8B1A4A"/>
<point at="fm-P" p="(-0.674,-1.165)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="fm-fP0" p="(7.35,0.25)" fill="#8B1A4A"/>
<point at="fm-fP" p="(6.55,0.25)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<label at="fm-disk-lab" anchor="(-1.0,2.0)" alignment="n">
<m>x^2+y^2\le 1</m>
</label>
<label at="fm-x" anchor="(2.1,-0.15)" alignment="se"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="fm-y" anchor="(0.15,2.8)" alignment="e"><m>y</m></label>
<label at="fm-z" anchor="(9.15,0.25)" alignment="e"><m>z</m></label>
<label at="fm-P0-lab" anchor="(0.883,1.103)" alignment="nw" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>P_0(0.5,0.5)</m>
</label>
<label at="fm-P-lab" anchor="(-0.674,-1.03)" alignment="n" color="#0072B2">
<m>P(x,y)</m>
</label>
<label at="fm-fP0-lab" anchor="(7.45,-0.05)" alignment="se" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>f(0.5,0.5)=0.5</m>
</label>
<label at="fm-fP-lab" anchor="(6.4,0.5)" alignment="nw" color="#0072B2">
<m>z=f(x,y)</m>
</label>
<label at="fm-f1" anchor="(6.85,0.55)" alignment="n" color="#7F7F7F">
<m>f</m>
</label>
<label at="fm-f2" anchor="(6.35,-0.25)" alignment="s" color="#7F7F7F">
<m>f</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="On the left, a shaded disk in the x y plane represents the domain, the set of points with x squared plus y squared at most one. Two points are marked in it: P nought at zero point five, zero point five, and a general point P. On the right is a horizontal z axis carrying the two output values. Dashed grey arrows labelled f run from each input point to its output value.">
  <annotation ref="fm-disk" text="The domain of f: the disk of radius one, consisting of all points with x squared plus y squared at most one."/>
  <annotation ref="fm-P0" text="The sample input point P nought with coordinates zero point five, zero point five."/>
  <annotation ref="fm-fP0" text="Its output, f of zero point five and zero point five, which equals zero point five, marked on the z axis."/>
  <annotation ref="fm-P" text="A general input point P with coordinates x and y."/>
  <annotation ref="fm-fP" text="Its output z equals f of x y on the z axis."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
If we repeat this process for all the points inside the disk, we have a
set of points in 3D of the form <m>P(x, y, f(x,y))</m>, which represent a
surface known as a <term>paraboloid</term>. The surface is the
<term>graph</term> of the function, and it is drawn in
<xref ref="fig-fsv-paraboloid"/>. Notice that the point
<m>P_0(0.5,0.5)</m> of the domain sits in the <m>xy</m>-plane, while the
corresponding point of the graph is <m>(0.5, 0.5, 0.5)</m>, directly
above it.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-fsv-paraboloid">
<caption>
The graph of <m>z = f(x,y) = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m> over the disk
<m>x^2+y^2 \le 1</m>. The point <m>P_0(0.5,0.5,0)</m> of the domain lies
in the <m>xy</m>-plane and the corresponding point
<m>P(0.5,0.5,0.5)</m> lies on the surface directly above it.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
A dome shaped surface, the graph of z equals one minus x squared minus y
squared, sitting above a shaded disk in the x y plane. A dashed vertical
segment joins the point P nought in the disk to the point P on the
surface directly above it.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=11cm, height=8.5cm,
  view={-35}{20},
  xmin=-1.35, xmax=1.35, ymin=-1.35, ymax=1.35, zmin=0, zmax=1.15,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  xtick={-1,0,1}, ytick={-1,0,1}, ztick={0,0.5,1},
  clip=false,
]
% the disk in the xy-plane
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=gray!35, draw=gray!45,
          fill opacity=0.35, draw opacity=0.3,
          domain=0:1, y domain=0:360, samples=12, samples y=37]
  ({x*cos(y)},{x*sin(y)},{0});
% the paraboloid z = 1 - x^2 - y^2 over the disk
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=gray!55, draw=gray!60,
          fill opacity=0.55, draw opacity=0.4,
          domain=0:1, y domain=0:360, samples=16, samples y=41,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({x*cos(y)},{x*sin(y)},{1-x^2});
% the rim of the disk
\addplot3[very thick, blue!65!black, domain=0:360, samples=90]
  ({cos(x)},{sin(x)},{0});
% P0 in the plane and P on the surface
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.7, color=purple!70!black]
  coordinates {(0.5,0.5,0)};
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.7, color=purple!70!black]
  coordinates {(0.5,0.5,0.5)};
\draw[gray, dashed, thick] (axis cs:0.5,0.5,0) -- (axis cs:0.5,0.5,0.5);
\node[purple!70!black, below] at (axis cs:0.5,0.52,-0.02) {$P_0$};
\node[purple!70!black, above right] at (axis cs:0.5,0.5,0.5) {$P$};
\node[blue!65!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.02,0.16)
  {$x^2+y^2=1$};
\node[gray!30!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.02,0.62)
  {$z=1-x^2-y^2$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-fsv-level-curves">
<title>Level Curves and Contour Maps</title>

<p>
Functions of two variables can be visualized by means of
<term>level curves</term>. These are curves that connect points where the
function <m>z = f(x,y)</m> takes on a given value <m>c</m>. As an
example, consider our previous example of
<m>z = f(x,y) = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m>. Setting <m>f(x,y) = c</m> gives
<md xml:id="eq-fsv-level-curves" number="yes">
1 - x^2 - y^2 = c
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
x^2 + y^2 = 1 - c,
</md>
so each level curve is a circle centered at the origin, of radius
<m>\sqrt{1-c}</m>. Taking
<m>c = -2, -1, 0, \tfrac14, \tfrac12</m> gives
<md>
<mrow>c = -2 \amp\;\Rightarrow\; x^2+y^2 = 3
      \amp\qquad c = \tfrac12 \amp\;\Rightarrow\; x^2+y^2 = \tfrac12</mrow>
<mrow>c = -1 \amp\;\Rightarrow\; x^2+y^2 = 2
      \amp\qquad c = \tfrac34 \amp\;\Rightarrow\; x^2+y^2 = \tfrac14</mrow>
<mrow>c = 0 \amp\;\Rightarrow\; x^2+y^2 = 1 \amp \amp</mrow>
</md>
<xref ref="fig-fsv-level-curves-3d"/> shows these curves drawn on the
surface itself: each one is the intersection of the graph with a
horizontal plane <m>z = c</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-fsv-level-curves-3d">
<caption>
Level curves of <m>f(x,y) = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m> drawn on the surface. The
curve at height <m>c</m> is the intersection of the graph with the
horizontal plane <m>z = c</m>; higher values of <m>c</m> give smaller
circles.
</caption>
<image width="76%">
<description>
<p>
A dome shaped surface with several horizontal circles drawn on it at
different heights, each in a different colour. The circles are small near
the top of the dome and grow larger further down.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=11.5cm, height=9.5cm,
  view={-35}{18},
  xmin=-1.9, xmax=1.9, ymin=-1.9, ymax=1.9, zmin=-2.4, zmax=1.3,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  xtick={-1,0,1}, ytick={-1,0,1}, ztick={1,0,-1,-2},
  clip=false,
]
% the surface, drawn down to z = -2.2
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=gray!45, draw=gray!55,
          fill opacity=0.42, draw opacity=0.3,
          domain=0:1.79, y domain=0:360, samples=13, samples y=31,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({x*cos(y)},{x*sin(y)},{1-x^2});
% level curves at c = 3/4, 1/2, 0, -1, -2  (radii sqrt(1-c))
\addplot3[very thick, orange, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({0.5*cos(x)},{0.5*sin(x)},{0.75});
\addplot3[very thick, purple!70!black, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({0.7071*cos(x)},{0.7071*sin(x)},{0.5});
\addplot3[very thick, cyan!60!black, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({cos(x)},{sin(x)},{0});
\addplot3[very thick, green!50!black, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({1.4142*cos(x)},{1.4142*sin(x)},{-1});
\addplot3[very thick, black, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({1.7321*cos(x)},{1.7321*sin(x)},{-2});
% labels
\node[orange, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.02,0.86)
  {$z=3/4$};
\node[purple!70!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.02,0.76)
  {$z=1/2$};
\node[cyan!60!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.02,0.66)
  {$z=0$};
\node[green!50!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.02,0.44)
  {$z=-1$};
\node[black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.02,0.22)
  {$z=-2$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
From the projection of the level curves into the <m>xy</m>-plane we
derive the so-called <term>contour map</term> (or
<term>contour plot</term>) of the function, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-fsv-contour-map"/>. A contour map is a flat picture that
still records the shape of the surface: where the contours are close
together the surface is steep, and where they are far apart it is
gentle.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-fsv-contour-map">
<caption>
The contour map of <m>f(x,y) = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m>, obtained by projecting
the level curves of <xref ref="fig-fsv-level-curves-3d"/> down into the
<m>xy</m>-plane. Each circle is labelled with the value <m>c</m> of the
function along it.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-fsv-contour">
<diagram dimensions="(360,360)" margins="12">
<definition>c1(t) = (0.5*cos(t), 0.5*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>c2(t) = (0.7071*cos(t), 0.7071*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>c3(t) = (1*cos(t), 1*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>c4(t) = (1.4142*cos(t), 1.4142*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>c5(t) = (1.7321*cos(t), 1.7321*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.1,-2.1,2.1,2.1)">
<axes at="ct-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-c1" function="c1" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#E69F00" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-c2" function="c2" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-c3" function="c3" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-c4" function="c4" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-c5" function="c5" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="black" thickness="2.5"/>
<label at="ct-l1" anchor="(0.36,0.36)" alignment="ne" color="#E69F00">
<m>c=\tfrac34</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l2" anchor="(0.50,-0.50)" alignment="se" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>c=\tfrac12</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l3" anchor="(-0.707,0.707)" alignment="nw" color="#0072B2">
<m>c=0</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l4" anchor="(-1.0,-1.0)" alignment="sw" color="#009E73">
<m>c=-1</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l5" anchor="(0,1.7321)" alignment="n">
<m>c=-2</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Five concentric circles centred at the origin in the x y plane, each drawn in a different colour and labelled with a value of c. The smallest, in amber, is c equals three quarters; going outwards they are c equals one half, c equals zero, c equals negative one, and the largest, in black, is c equals negative two.">
  <annotation ref="ct-c1" text="The level curve where f equals three quarters, a circle of radius one half. It is the innermost circle, so the largest values of f occur near the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-c2" text="The level curve where f equals one half, a circle of radius one over root two."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-c3" text="The level curve where f equals zero, the unit circle."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-c4" text="The level curve where f equals negative one, a circle of radius root two."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-c5" text="The level curve where f equals negative two, a circle of radius root three. The circles spread further apart as c decreases, which tells us the surface is steepest near the rim."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-fsv-level-curves">
<title>Sketching Level Curves</title>
<statement>
<p>
Sketch some level curves of the function
<m>g(x,y) = x^2 + \dfrac{y^2}{4}</m>.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Setting <m>g(x,y) = c</m> gives
<md>
x^2 + \frac{y^2}{4} = c,
</md>
which for <m>c \gt 0</m> is an ellipse centered at the origin. For
instance, for <m>c = 1</m> we have <m>x^2 + \frac{y^2}{4} = 1</m>, which
is an ellipse with <m>a = 1</m> and <m>b = 2</m>: it meets the
<m>x</m>-axis at <m>\pm 1</m> and the <m>y</m>-axis at <m>\pm 2</m>. In
general the level curve <m>g = c</m> is the ellipse with semi-axes
<m>\sqrt c</m> in the <m>x</m>-direction and <m>2\sqrt c</m> in the
<m>y</m>-direction, so the curves are stretched twice as far along the
<m>y</m>-axis as along the <m>x</m>-axis.
<xref ref="fig-fsv-elliptic-contour"/> shows the level curves for
several values of <m>c</m>.
</p>
<p>
The surface <m>z = g(x,y) = x^2 + \frac{y^2}{4}</m> itself is shown in
<xref ref="fig-fsv-elliptic-paraboloid"/>. It is known as an
<term>elliptic paraboloid</term>, since its intersections with the
planes <m>x = c</m> and <m>y = c</m> are parabolas, whereas its
intersections with the planes <m>z = c</m> are ellipses.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-fsv-elliptic-contour">
<caption>
Level curves of <m>g(x,y) = x^2 + \frac{y^2}{4}</m> for
<m>c = 0.1, 0.2, \ldots, 1</m>. Each is an ellipse twice as long in the
<m>y</m>-direction as in the <m>x</m>-direction.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-fsv-elliptic">
<diagram dimensions="(330,420)" margins="12">
<definition>e1(t) = (0.3162*cos(t), 0.6325*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>e2(t) = (0.4472*cos(t), 0.8944*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>e3(t) = (0.5477*cos(t), 1.0954*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>e4(t) = (0.6325*cos(t), 1.2649*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>e5(t) = (0.7071*cos(t), 1.4142*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>e6(t) = (0.8367*cos(t), 1.6733*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>e7(t) = (1*cos(t), 2*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.45,-2.35,1.45,2.35)">
<axes at="el-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="el-e1" function="e1" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#00441B" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="el-e2" function="e2" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#0B6B3A" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="el-e3" function="e3" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#2C9153" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="el-e4" function="e4" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#57B36B" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="el-e5" function="e5" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#8ACE88" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="el-e6" function="e6" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#BFE3A6" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="el-e7" function="e7" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#E5D93C" thickness="2.5"/>
<label at="el-lab" anchor="(1.05,2.05)" alignment="ne" color="#B8AE20">
<m>c=1</m>
</label>
<label at="el-lab2" anchor="(0.34,0.68)" alignment="ne" color="#00441B">
<m>c=0.1</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A family of nested ellipses centred at the origin, all twice as tall as they are wide. They are shaded from dark green for the innermost, where c equals nought point one, out to yellow for the outermost, where c equals one.">
  <annotation ref="el-e1" text="The innermost level curve, where g equals nought point one."/>
  <annotation ref="el-e7" text="The outermost level curve, where g equals one. It is the ellipse x squared plus y squared over four equals one, crossing the x axis at plus and minus one and the y axis at plus and minus two."/>
  <annotation ref="el-axes" text="The ellipses are stretched along the y axis because the y term is divided by four, so reaching a given height takes twice the distance in y that it does in x."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-fsv-elliptic-paraboloid">
<caption>
The elliptic paraboloid <m>z = g(x,y) = x^2 + \frac{y^2}{4}</m>. Slicing
it with a horizontal plane <m>z = c</m> produces the ellipses of
<xref ref="fig-fsv-elliptic-contour"/>.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
A bowl shaped surface opening upwards, wider in the y direction than in
the x direction, with several horizontal elliptical rings drawn on it at
different heights.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=11cm, height=8.5cm,
  view={-40}{18},
  xmin=-1.25, xmax=1.25, ymin=-2.5, ymax=2.5, zmin=0, zmax=1.2,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  xtick={-1,0,1}, ytick={-2,0,2}, ztick={0,0.5,1},
  clip=false,
]
% surface z = x^2 + y^2/4, drawn in "elliptic polar" coordinates so the
% domain is exactly the region where z <= 1
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=green!45!black, draw=green!35!black,
          fill opacity=0.35, draw opacity=0.35,
          domain=0:1, y domain=0:360, samples=13, samples y=31,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({x*cos(y)},{2*x*sin(y)},{x^2});
% a few level ellipses on the surface
\addplot3[very thick, green!25!black, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({0.4472*cos(x)},{0.8944*sin(x)},{0.2});
\addplot3[very thick, green!45!black, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({0.6325*cos(x)},{1.2649*sin(x)},{0.4});
\addplot3[very thick, green!60!black, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({0.7746*cos(x)},{1.5492*sin(x)},{0.6});
\addplot3[very thick, yellow!70!black, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({cos(x)},{2*sin(x)},{1});
\node[green!35!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.02,0.72)
  {$z=x^2+y^2/4$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-fsv-level-surfaces">
<title>More Than Two Variables: Level Surfaces</title>

<p>
It is not possible to draw the graph of a function of more than two
variables. The graph of a function <m>f(x,y,z)</m> would consist of the
set of points <m>(x, y, z, f(x,y,z))</m> in four-dimensional space
<m>\R^4</m>. However, it is possible to draw the
<term>level surfaces</term> of a function of three variables
<m>f(x,y,z)</m>. These are the surfaces with equation
<md xml:id="eq-fsv-level-surface" number="yes">
f(x,y,z) = c.
</md>
</p>

<p>
For example, the level surfaces of
<md>
f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + z^2
</md>
are the spheres with equation <m>x^2+y^2+z^2 = c</m>, drawn in
<xref ref="fig-fsv-level-spheres"/> for <m>c = 1, 4, 9</m>. Each sphere
collects the points at which <m>f</m> takes one fixed value; as <m>c</m>
grows the spheres grow with it. For functions of four or more variables
we can no longer visualize the graph or the level surfaces, and we must
rely on intuition developed through the study of functions of two and
three variables.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-fsv-level-spheres">
<caption>
The level surfaces of <m>f(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2+z^2</m> are spheres. Shown
here are <m>x^2+y^2+z^2 = 1</m>, <m>x^2+y^2+z^2 = 4</m>, and
<m>x^2+y^2+z^2 = 9</m>, cut away so the inner ones are visible.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
Three concentric spheres of radii one, two and three, drawn cut away so
that the smaller spheres inside can be seen. Each sphere is a level
surface on which the function x squared plus y squared plus z squared is
constant.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0, >=stealth]
% three cut-away hemispheres, drawn in the picture plane
\begin{scope}
  % outer, radius 3 -> drawn at 2.4cm
  \fill[cyan!30, opacity=0.55] (-2.4,0) arc (180:360:2.4cm) arc (0:180:2.4cm and 0.62cm);
  \draw[cyan!55!black] (-2.4,0) arc (180:360:2.4cm);
  \draw[cyan!55!black] (-2.4,0) arc (180:0:2.4cm and 0.62cm);
  % middle, radius 2 -> 1.6cm
  \fill[orange!35, opacity=0.6] (-1.6,0) arc (180:360:1.6cm) arc (0:180:1.6cm and 0.42cm);
  \draw[orange!60!black] (-1.6,0) arc (180:360:1.6cm);
  \draw[orange!60!black] (-1.6,0) arc (180:0:1.6cm and 0.42cm);
  % inner, radius 1 -> 0.8cm
  \fill[green!45, opacity=0.75] (-0.8,0) arc (180:360:0.8cm) arc (0:180:0.8cm and 0.2cm);
  \draw[green!45!black] (-0.8,0) arc (180:360:0.8cm);
  \draw[green!45!black] (-0.8,0) arc (180:0:0.8cm and 0.2cm);
\end{scope}
% axes
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,3.1) node[above] {$z$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (3.3,-0.85) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-2.9,-1.3) node[below left] {$x$};
% labels
\node[green!35!black, anchor=west]  at (2.6,1.9) {$x^2+y^2+z^2=1$};
\node[orange!60!black, anchor=west] at (2.6,1.35) {$x^2+y^2+z^2=4$};
\node[cyan!55!black, anchor=west]   at (2.6,0.8) {$x^2+y^2+z^2=9$};
\draw[gray, thin] (2.55,1.9) -- (0.42,0.42);
\draw[gray, thin] (2.55,1.35) -- (1.05,0.95);
\draw[gray, thin] (2.55,0.8) -- (1.75,1.5);
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-fsv-level-surfaces">
<title>Level Surfaces of a Function of Three Variables</title>
<statement>
<p>
Describe the level surfaces of <m>g(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 - z^2</m>.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
The level surface for <m>c = 0</m> is
<md>
x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = 0,
</md>
which is a cone: solving for <m>z</m> gives
<m>z = \pm\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>, a double cone with vertex at the origin.
</p>
<p>
For <m>c \neq 0</m>, the level surfaces are the hyperboloids
<md>
x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = c.
</md>
The hyperboloid has <em>one sheet</em> if <m>c \gt 0</m>, since then
<m>x^2+y^2 = c + z^2 \ge c \gt 0</m> is satisfied for every value of
<m>z</m>, and the surface is connected. It has <em>two sheets</em> if
<m>c \lt 0</m>, since then <m>z^2 = x^2+y^2-c \ge -c \gt 0</m> forces
<m>|z| \ge \sqrt{-c}</m>, so no point of the surface lies in the slab
between the two sheets. The three cases are drawn in
<xref ref="fig-fsv-hyperboloids"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-fsv-hyperboloids">
<caption>
Level surfaces of <m>g(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2-z^2</m>. For <m>c \gt 0</m> the
surface is a hyperboloid of one sheet, for <m>c = 0</m> it is a cone, and
for <m>c \lt 0</m> it is a hyperboloid of two sheets.
</caption>
<image width="98%">
<description>
<p>
Three surfaces side by side. On the left a hyperboloid of one sheet, a
connected surface pinched in at the middle like a cooling tower. In the
middle a double cone meeting at a point at the origin. On the right a
hyperboloid of two sheets, consisting of two separate bowls, one opening
upwards above the origin and one opening downwards below it.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
% ============ left: one sheet, c > 0 ============
\begin{axis}[
  name=one,
  width=6.4cm, height=7.0cm,
  view={-35}{16},
  xmin=-2.2, xmax=2.2, ymin=-2.2, ymax=2.2, zmin=-1.8, zmax=1.8,
  hide axis, clip=false,
]
% x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = 1  ->  r = sqrt(1 + z^2)
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=cyan!35, draw=cyan!55!black,
          fill opacity=0.55, draw opacity=0.45,
          domain=-1.5:1.5, y domain=0:360, samples=16, samples y=41,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({sqrt(1+x^2)*cos(y)},{sqrt(1+x^2)*sin(y)},{x});
\node at (axis cs:0,0,-2.5) {$g(x,y,z)=c\;\;(c>0)$};
\end{axis}
% ============ middle: the cone, c = 0 ============
\begin{axis}[
  name=cone, at={(one.east)}, anchor=west, xshift=2mm,
  width=6.4cm, height=7.0cm,
  view={-35}{16},
  xmin=-2.2, xmax=2.2, ymin=-2.2, ymax=2.2, zmin=-1.8, zmax=1.8,
  hide axis, clip=false,
]
% x^2 + y^2 = z^2  ->  r = |z|
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=orange!30, draw=orange!60!black,
          fill opacity=0.55, draw opacity=0.45,
          domain=-1.7:1.7, y domain=0:360, samples=16, samples y=41,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({abs(x)*cos(y)},{abs(x)*sin(y)},{x});
\node at (axis cs:0,0,-2.5) {$g(x,y,z)=0$};
\end{axis}
% ============ right: two sheets, c < 0 ============
\begin{axis}[
  name=two, at={(cone.east)}, anchor=west, xshift=2mm,
  width=6.4cm, height=7.0cm,
  view={-35}{16},
  xmin=-2.2, xmax=2.2, ymin=-2.2, ymax=2.2, zmin=-1.8, zmax=1.8,
  hide axis, clip=false,
]
% x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = -1  ->  r = sqrt(z^2 - 1),  |z| >= 1
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=green!35, draw=green!45!black,
          fill opacity=0.6, draw opacity=0.45,
          domain=1:1.75, y domain=0:360, samples=12, samples y=41,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({sqrt(x^2-1)*cos(y)},{sqrt(x^2-1)*sin(y)},{x});
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=green!35, draw=green!45!black,
          fill opacity=0.6, draw opacity=0.45,
          domain=-1.75:-1, y domain=0:360, samples=12, samples y=41,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({sqrt(x^2-1)*cos(y)},{sqrt(x^2-1)*sin(y)},{x});
\node at (axis cs:0,0,-2.5) {$g(x,y,z)=c\;\;(c<0)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

</section>
